#!/usr/bin/env bash
# commit-msg — require a delegation trailer on every commit: one line naming who did the work,
# an agent or a person, so delegation is recordable and the question can be settled from data.
#
# WHY A HOOK AND NOT A RULE: a rule that depends on someone remembering to follow it is not a
# mechanism — written conventions here have failed silently before. The hook makes the omission
# loud at the moment it happens.
#
# WHAT IT MEASURES: "no delegation trace" is not the same fact as "not delegated" — while no trace
# convention exists, both readings survive the same evidence, so a delegation rule cannot be
# settled either way. That unfalsifiability is the defect this hook removes: the trailer makes the
# distinction recordable.
#
# ★ THE VALUES ARE DELIBERATELY FREE-TEXT. This is the measurement phase — closing the carve-out
# list now would presuppose the answer being measured, and a list ending in "or other work" would
# pass every commit by construction (zero information). Let the real reasons accumulate, THEN
# decide which are legitimate.
#
# It REJECTS. It never auto-fills a trailer — a hook that invents the datum it is collecting is
# the dry-run-that-writes defect.
#
# Install: guard/hooks/install.sh   ·   Read the results: guard/delegation_tally.py
msg_file="$1"
msg=$(cat "$msg_file")

# Merges and reverts are mechanical, not authored work — nothing to attribute.
case "$msg" in
  "Merge "*|"Revert "*|"fixup!"*|"squash!"*) exit 0 ;;
esac

# The two accepted trailer names are TOOL-NEUTRAL on purpose: an adopter should be able to record
# who did the work without adopting anyone else's vocabulary for their agents or subsystems.
if printf '%s\n' "$msg" | grep -qE '^(Delegated-to|Authored-directly):[[:space:]]*[^[:space:]]'; then
  exit 0
fi

cat >&2 <<'EOF'

  ⛔ COMMIT REJECTED — no delegation trailer.

  Add exactly one line to the commit message:

      Delegated-to: <who>            e.g. another agent · a teammate · a code-writing model · a contractor
      Authored-directly: <why>       e.g. integration/wiring · guard code · correspondence · a record update

  This is the measurement phase, not an enforced policy — the values are free text on purpose,
  so the real distribution decides the rule instead of a guess made now.

  If a commit genuinely does not fit either, say so in the value rather than picking the closer one:
      Authored-directly: unclear — mixed, mostly reviewing a delegated draft
  An honest "unclear" is a datum. A tidied-up label is a fabricated one.

EOF
exit 1
